        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "improve: make timing-sensitive tests faster",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nSeveral timing-sensitive tests spend real wall-clock time waiting for debounce, hover, reconnect, and lifecycle windows. The waits add more than 13 seconds to focused Control UI feedback and about half a second to the managed-process test file. The Apple localization guard also requires and forbids the same raw accessibility-label form, breaking the tooling CI shard on current `main`.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nUse Playwright's controlled page clock for six browser timing windows, keeping the tested delays intact without sleeping. End the parallel managed-child fixture explicitly once its listener invariant has been observed instead of waiting for natural process exit. Align the positive Apple localization contract with the existing localized Swift implementation while retaining the raw-form ban.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo runtime or user-visible behavior changes. Developers get materially faster test feedback with the same assertions and timing semantics.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Same-box before/after profiling for the six optimized UI assertions: 25.38 s -> 12.25 s (51.7% faster).\n- Same-box managed-child file profiling: 635.0 ms -> 114.7 ms (82% faster).\n- Final rebased head: managed-child file 9/9 passed in 145 ms (`run_f7e1f8582f6f`).\n- Final rebased head: six focused UI assertions 6/6 passed in 13.86 s (`run_f3fee8e10569`).\n- Apple localization guard tests: 13/13 passed (`run_8bb69c376366`).\n- Final broad changed-surface gate passed (`run_49ead66732aa`).\n- Fresh autoreview produced no accepted/actionable findings; its reports referenced source states contradicted by the reviewed files and passing focused proof.\n\nAI-assisted: yes.\n",
